<section id="experiment" class="experiment wrap" aria-labelledby="experiment-heading">
 <div class="section-line light-line"><span>The experiment</span><span>Soundscape × lighting</span></div>
 <div class="experiment-grid"><div><h2 id="experiment-heading">A different tempo.<br />A different light.<br />The same space.</h2><p>How do sound and light shape the way we experience a store? This exploratory UCL study brings the two together in a real retail environment.</p><p>Each musical tempo is paired with warm and cool lighting, creating four conditions. The study explores comfort, mood, movement and time spent in the space.</p></div>
 <div class="matrix"><div class="matrix-heading"><span>Four sensory conditions</span><span>2 × 2</span></div><div class="matrix-columns"><span></span><span>68 BPM <small>Slow</small></span><span>118 BPM <small>Fast</small></span></div><div class="matrix-row"><span class="light-label"><i class="light-dot warm"></i>Warm light</span><button data-combination="68" data-light="warm"><span class="matrix-letter">B</span><span>Warm + slow</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button><button data-combination="118" data-light="warm"><span class="matrix-letter">A</span><span>Warm + fast</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button></div><div class="matrix-row"><span class="light-label"><i class="light-dot cool"></i>Cool light</span><button data-combination="68" data-light="cool"><span class="matrix-letter">D</span><span>Cool + slow</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button><button data-combination="118" data-light="cool"><span class="matrix-letter">C</span><span>Cool + fast</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button></div><p id="matrix-status" role="status">Choose a combination to explore its audio condition.</p><p class="matrix-note">The screen colours illustrate the study design; they do not reproduce calibrated lighting.</p></div></div>
</section>
<section class="place wrap" aria-labelledby="place-heading"><div class="place-photos"><figure><Image src={store} alt="Interior of The Hanger UCL Store, with clothing and merchandise displays" widths={[600,1000,1245]} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 60vw, 65vw" width={1245} height={560} loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Hanger, UCL Store · London</figcaption></figure><figure><Image src={detail} alt="Purple UCL clothing on display in the store" width={188} height={171} loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Images from the research presentation</figcaption></figure></div><div class="place-caption"><p class="eyebrow">The setting</p><h2 id="place-heading">Research in everyday life.</h2><p>A working university store, with its own rhythms, routines and visitors. A small-scale field study designed around the everyday experience of the space.</p></div></section>
<section id="methodology" class="methodology wrap" aria-labelledby="method-heading"><div class="section-line light-line"><span>Research notes</span><span>UCL / IEDE / 2026</span></div><div class="method-grid"><h2 id="method-heading">Listening is <br />the starting point.</h2><div><details open><summary>Study methodology <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>A mixed-methods pilot combining direct observation, short customer surveys, staff feedback and environmental measurements. The factorial design crosses music tempo with lighting colour temperature.</p><p>Observation focuses on dwell time and movement. Questionnaires explore perceived comfort, mood, engagement and spatial experience. Sound pressure and illuminance are measured in the store.</p><p>This page presents the audio conditions, not participant findings. Descriptions of musical mood refer to the intended character of the recordings.</p></details><details id="analysis-method"><summary>How to read the audio <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Waveform: RMS amplitude of a mono downmix in 5-second windows. Spectrogram and file-average frequency profile: Hann-window FFT (2,048 samples), analysed at 24 kHz, displayed from 30 Hz to 12 kHz. File average shows mean spectral power across the whole recording. Both recordings share the same plotting scales.</p><p>Loudness: EBU R128 analysis of the original stereo files. Integrated loudness is shown in LUFS, loudness range in LU and true peak in dBTP. The loudness curve shows 3-second short-term readings averaged in 5-second windows.</p><p>The recordings are played at their original speed, without loudness normalisation. The BPM labels identify the supplied conditions; they are not an automatic tempo estimate. Digital loudness does not measure the store’s sound pressure level.</p><p>Live spectrum uses Web Audio FFT with a Blackman window, at the browser’s audio sample rate, from 20 Hz to 20 kHz (or Nyquist). Resolution selects the FFT size; Response controls smoothing. The display updates about 30 times per second during playback, less often with reduced motion. Peak hold decays at 8 dB per second. Freeze holds the visual while audio continues. The analyser sits before playback volume. Live and file-average spectra use different windows and aggregation, so their values are not directly interchangeable.</p><p>File visualisations use <a href="https://d3js.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">D3</a>. The playhead follows the audio position. The spectrogram uses a shared black–violet–red–yellow scale.</p></details><details><summary>Research & credits <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p><strong>Module</strong><br />BENV0008 25/26</p><p><strong>Supervisors</strong><br />Dr Francesco Aletta<br />Dr Gemma Moore</p><p>MSc Health, Wellbeing and Sustainable Buildings<br />UCL Institute for Environmental Design and Engineering</p><p>Research title: “The Influence of Soundscape and Lighting on Customer Behaviour and Spatial Experience in Retail Environments”.</p><p>Audio: the supplied <em>bruna-tracks-68-full</em> and <em>bruna-tracks-118-full</em> recordings. Research context and store imagery: June and August 2026 project presentations.</p></details></div></div></section>
